"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][14]={"school_physics_en:M14-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M14-L1 – Motion with constant acceleration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Motion models and mechanics in the introductory phase<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A cart begins from rest. Its velocity increases by the same amount\nevery second. What shape does its position–time graph have?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Acceleration<\/strong> is the rate of change of velocity:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=\\Delta v/\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmeasured in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen acceleration is <strong>constant<\/strong> along a straight line,\nafter time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasured from the chosen start:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(t)=v_0+at,\\qquad x(t)=x_0+v_0t+\\tfrac12at^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Eliminating time,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^2=v_0^2+2as<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=x-x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis signed displacement. Both positive and negative values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare possible; signs depend on the chosen axis. Negative acceleration\ndoes not invariably mean slowing down: for an object travelling in the\nnegative direction it can <strong>increase<\/strong> the speed.<\/p>\n<p>On a <strong>velocity–time graph<\/strong>, slope represents\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand signed area represents displacement. A <strong>position–time\ngraph<\/strong> is parabolic when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe tangent slope gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMotion at constant velocity, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nis the straight-line special case. In every question identify initial\nposition and velocity, signs and the modelled interval before\ncalculating.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_082_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parabolic position–time graph and linear velocity–time graph for 3 m/s² over 4 s, with area 24 m.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parabolic position–time graph and linear\nvelocity–time graph for 3 m/s² over 4 s, with area 24 m.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>At constant acceleration, position is quadratic in time while\nvelocity is linear.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A model cart starts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom rest\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nand accelerates constantly at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFinal velocity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0+3\\cdot4=12\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nposition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\frac12\\cdot3\\cdot4^2=24\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe triangular area below the velocity–time line is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12\\cdot4\\cdot12=24\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconfirming the displacement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Graph activity:<\/strong> Construct a synthetic data table for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,1,2,3,4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPlot position–time, velocity–time and acceleration–time graphs\nseparately, with units. Compare graph shape, slopes and areas;\nexplicitly label the values as model data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate displacement for the same interval and model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A cart starts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=-2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen does it first stop?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can speed increase when acceleration is negative? Give an\nexample.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=2\\cdot3+\\tfrac12\\cdot2\\cdot3^2=15\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0=10-2t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=-2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=-1\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nresult in a growing speed while velocity becomes more negative.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The constant-acceleration model fails when forces change\nsubstantially with time. A small set of readings cannot prove exactly\nconstant acceleration; initial conditions and measurement uncertainties\nmatter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L2<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/332/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26 onward:\nintroductory phase, mechanics, kinematics, dynamics and circular motion;\nmandate is state-specific · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3.1:\nelective introductory-phase topics – projectile motion, experimentation\nand circular motion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-4-motion-with-constant-acceleration\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 3.4 Motion with Constant Acceleration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 3.4: kinematic equations and constant-acceleration assumptions ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M14-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M14-L2 – Free fall and projectile motion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Motion models and mechanics in the introductory phase<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A ball travels horizontally past the edge of a table. Why does it\nfollow a curved path rather than falling first and moving horizontally\nafterwards?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Near Earth, gravitational acceleration is often approximated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\approx9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the following school calculations we explicitly use the simplified\nvalue\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTake horizontal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand upward-positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith <strong>horizontal launch<\/strong> and negligible air\nresistance,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>g<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=x_0+v_{0x}t,\\qquad y(t)=y_0-\\tfrac12gt^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Horizontal velocity is constant\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>);\ndownward vertical velocity grows in magnitude, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_y=-gt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese components act <strong>simultaneously<\/strong> and share the same\ntime\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe trajectory is parabolic. For a body <strong>released from\nrest<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{0x}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand fall time from height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\sqrt{2h/g}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this ideal model, a body released from the same height at the same\ninstant lands alongside one launched horizontally.<\/p>\n<p>These heights and ranges are mathematical examples, not instructions\nto perform hazardous projectile experiments.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_083_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parabolic trajectory at five times for ideal horizontal launch from 20 m with 6 m/s horizontal speed.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parabolic trajectory at five times for\nideal horizontal launch from 20 m with 6 m/s horizontal\nspeed.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Horizontal and vertical motion occur simultaneously.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine a horizontal launch from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{0x}=6\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUse the simplified\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0=20-\\frac12\\cdot10t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe horizontal range is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=6\\cdot2=12\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nJust before impact,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_y=-20\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x=6\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe model activity:<\/strong> In a spreadsheet list\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,0.5,1,1.5,2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=6t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=20-5t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand plot the trajectory. Identify the numbers as theoretical simulation\nvalues; do not reproduce a launch from height.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the fall time from rest for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How far does a horizontally moving object travel in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_x=4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is its horizontal acceleration when air drag is neglected?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do a released body and one launched horizontally land at the\nsame time in the ideal model?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\sqrt{2\\cdot5/10}=1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=4\\cdot1.5=6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both start with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{0y}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the same height and have identical vertical acceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>In reality, drag and buoyancy affect trajectories and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not exactly constant everywhere. The ideal parabola is an\napproximation and does not justify an unsafe experiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L1<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L3<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/332/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26 onward:\nintroductory phase, mechanics, kinematics, dynamics and circular motion;\nmandate is state-specific · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3.1:\nelective introductory-phase topics – projectile motion, experimentation\nand circular motion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-3-projectile-motion\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 4.3 Projectile Motion<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 4.3:\nseparate horizontal and vertical components of projectile motion · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M14-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M14-L3 – Newton’s laws in multistep situations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Motion models and mechanics in the introductory phase<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two people pull a crate in opposite directions. Does the mere\npresence of a pull to the right guarantee acceleration to the right?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Newton’s three laws provide a dynamical model for speeds far below\nthe speed of light. <strong>Inertia:<\/strong> With zero net external\nforce, velocity remains constant, including rest.\n<strong>Dynamics:<\/strong> The vector sum of <strong>external<\/strong>\nforces equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum\\vec F=m\\vec a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Interaction:<\/strong> If object A pushes or pulls B, then B\nexerts an equal and opposite force on A. These forces act on\n<strong>different objects<\/strong> and therefore never cancel on the\nfree-body diagram of just one object.<\/p>\n<p>Start a problem by selecting the <strong>system<\/strong> and drawing\nevery force acting on it. Horizontally, one may write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\mathrm{pull}-F_\\mathrm{friction}=ma_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nvertically, for a body on a level support, often\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N-mg=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMotion depends on the <strong>net<\/strong> force, not the largest\nindividual force. Forces that balance on one body are not automatically\na Newton-third-law pair.<\/p>\n<p>The simple form of the second law assumes a suitable approximately\n<strong>inertial reference frame<\/strong> and constant mass in the\nschool model. Accelerating and rotating frames need additional care.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_084_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Crate with 10 N pull, 2 N friction, weight and normal force, with 8 N net horizontal force.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Crate with 10 N pull, 2 N friction,\nweight and normal force, with 8 N net horizontal force.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Net external force produces acceleration; vertical forces balance\nin this model.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncrate is pulled right with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand experiences\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkinetic friction to the left. The net horizontal force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10-2=8\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=8/2=4\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWeight\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>19.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx19.6\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nand the equal normal force cancel only <strong>vertically<\/strong>. The\ncrate accelerates horizontally.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper and simulation activity:<\/strong> Draw free-body\ndiagrams for the same crate with pulls of 0 N, 2 N and 10 N while it is\nsliding against 2 N friction. Discuss why static friction changes with\napplied force and must not automatically be assigned a fixed 2 N before\nsliding begins.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=4\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand net force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are weight and the normal force on a stationary crate not an\naction–reaction pair?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An object moves with constant velocity. What is its net external\nforce?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A 9 N pull acts right, 4 N drag left, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=12/4=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both act on the same crate; interaction forces must act on different\nobjects.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum\\vec F=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the chosen inertial frame.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\mathrm{net}=5\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the right and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=5\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Friction values are model-dependent. When combining bodies into one\nsystem, identify which contact forces are internal and which are\nexternal.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L2<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L4<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/332/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26 onward:\nintroductory phase, mechanics, kinematics, dynamics and circular motion;\nmandate is state-specific · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-1-solving-problems-with-newtons-laws\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 6.1 Solving Problems with Newton’s Laws<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 6.1: free-body diagrams and net external forces · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M14-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M14-L4 – Circular motion and centripetal acceleration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Motion models and mechanics in the introductory phase<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A wheel turns at constant speed. The magnitude of its velocity stays\nconstant. Why is it still accelerating?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Velocity is a <strong>vector<\/strong>. During uniform circular motion\nits direction continually changes even if its magnitude is fixed.\nConsequently the object has <strong>centripetal acceleration<\/strong>\ntoward the circle’s centre:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>T<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\mathrm c=\\frac{v^2}{r}=\\omega^2r,\\qquad v=\\frac{2\\pi r}{T}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis radius,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe period and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\pi/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe angular speed. The required <strong>net radial force<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\mathrm c=ma_\\mathrm c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n“Centripetal force” is not an extra kind of force: a rope’s tension,\nfriction or gravity may supply the needed inward resultant.\n<strong>Velocity<\/strong> is tangent to the circle, whereas\n<strong>acceleration<\/strong> is radially inward and perpendicular to\nvelocity.<\/p>\n<p>If speed changes as well, tangential acceleration must also be\nincluded. An apparent outward “centrifugal force” arises in a rotating\nnon-inertial frame; it is not an additional external force acting on the\norbiting body in a stationary inertial frame.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_085_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Circular orbit with object on the right, tangent velocity upward and acceleration toward the centre.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Circular orbit with object on the right,\ntangent velocity upward and acceleration toward the centre.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Uniform circular motion keeps speed constant but continually\nchanges velocity direction.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An object travels in a circle of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncompleting a revolution in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=2\\pi\\cdot2/4=\\pi\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\approx3.14\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.93<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\mathrm c=v^2/r=\\pi^2/2\\approx4.93\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.47<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\mathrm c\\approx2.47\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe force arrow points toward the centre at each position.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper rotation model:<\/strong> Mark four equally spaced\npoints around a circle. At each point draw tangent velocity and radially\ninward acceleration. Label a possible physical cause of the inward\nforce. No fast-spinning equipment is required.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which direction does instantaneous velocity point during circular\nmotion?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles while radius is fixed, by what factor does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\mathrm c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\mathrm c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is centripetal force an extra independent interaction?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Along a tangent to the path.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It becomes four times larger because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\mathrm c\\propto v^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\mathrm c=4^2/2=8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. It denotes the inward resultant of real forces.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Nonuniform circular motion also has tangential acceleration; limited\nfriction or tension may end circular motion. The arrows in a diagram\nillustrate directions rather than exact force magnitudes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L3<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L5<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/332/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26 onward:\nintroductory phase, mechanics, kinematics, dynamics and circular motion;\nmandate is state-specific · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3.1:\nelective introductory-phase topics – projectile motion, experimentation\nand circular motion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-3-centripetal-force\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 6.3 Centripetal Force<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 6.3:\ncentripetal acceleration and inward net force · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M14-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M14-L5 – Forces on an inclined plane<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Motion models and mechanics in the introductory phase<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why is a heavy crate easier to hold on a gentle ramp than on a steep\none even though its weight stays the same?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>For a slope inclined by angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabove the horizontal, choose axes <strong>parallel<\/strong> and\n<strong>perpendicular<\/strong> to the surface. Weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>g<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec F_g=m\\vec g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalways points vertically down. Its components have magnitudes<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\parallel=mg\\sin\\alpha,\\qquad F_\\perp=mg\\cos\\alpha.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the body remains in contact and no other perpendicular forces act,\nthe normal force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=F_\\perp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDuring <strong>sliding<\/strong>, approximate kinetic friction by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\mathrm f=\\mu_kN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nopposite the sliding direction. For downhill sliding under gravity and\nkinetic friction alone,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ma=mg\\sin\\alpha-\\mu_kmg\\cos\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=g(\\sin\\alpha-\\mu_k\\cos\\alpha)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis only applies where the assumed direction of sliding is consistent\nwith the situation.<\/p>\n<p>For an initially <strong>stationary<\/strong> object, static friction\nmust be checked separately: it adjusts to oppose impending motion up to\nthe maximum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_sN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNever substitute kinetic friction for static friction or assume that the\nnormal force on a slope is always\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_086_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Incline showing vertical weight, downslope component and perpendicular outward normal reaction.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Incline showing vertical weight,\ndownslope component and perpendicular outward normal\nreaction.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Weight splits into along- and across-slope components; the normal\nforce is a separate interaction.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A crate of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nslides downhill on a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nslope. For a teaching calculation take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_k=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDownhill component:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\parallel=2\\cdot10\\cdot0.5=10\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNormal force:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>17.32<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=20\\cos30^\\circ\\approx17.32\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFriction:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.46<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\mathrm f\\approx3.46\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuphill. Net downhill force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.54<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.54\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.27<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\approx3.27\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Geometric model:<\/strong> Draw slopes of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>15<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConstruct the directions of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\parallel<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\perp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand compare their magnitudes using trigonometry. Do not release heavy\nrolling objects on unsecured ramps.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\parallel<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=3\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the normal force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder those conditions with no extra perpendicular forces.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which way does friction act while the crate slides downhill?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\mathrm f=\\mu_kN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot sufficient for a stationary crate?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\parallel=3\\cdot10\\cdot0.5=15\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>25.98<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=30\\cos30^\\circ\\approx25.98\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Kinetic friction acts uphill.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Static friction varies up to a limiting value, so its actual\nmagnitude need not equal kinetic friction.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Real friction depends on materials, surface condition and sometimes\nspeed. Without knowing whether motion has begun and what other forces\nact, its magnitude and direction cannot be assumed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L4<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L6<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/332/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26 onward:\nintroductory phase, mechanics, kinematics, dynamics and circular motion;\nmandate is state-specific · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-6-common-forces\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 5.6 Common Forces<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 5.6: gravity\ncomponents and normal force on an incline · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M14-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M14-L6 – Comparing friction and air-drag models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Motion models and mechanics in the introductory phase<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A paper sheet falls through air much more slowly than a metal sphere.\nWhy cannot one fixed friction force account for both?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Dry kinetic friction<\/strong> between solid surfaces is often\napproximated during sliding by the simple Coulomb model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\mathrm f\\approx\\mu_kN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithin that model its magnitude is largely independent of sliding speed\nand points against relative motion. <strong>Fluid drag<\/strong> in air\nor water, however, commonly depends on velocity. Two model regimes are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D=kv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor certain low-speed flows and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D=cv^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin others, where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the <strong>magnitude of velocity relative to the fluid<\/strong>. The\nactual drag force acts against that motion.<\/p>\n<p>The coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbundles properties such as fluid density, shape and cross-sectional area\nunder simplified conditions; in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D=cv^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nits unit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kg/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor downward-positive motion under gravity,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ma=mg-cv^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Terminal speed<\/strong> is reached when the forces balance:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\mathrm{term}=\\sqrt{mg/c}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At that point the body continues at constant speed; it does not stop\nin midair. A linear\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D=kv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodel would yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\mathrm{term}=mg/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nChoosing between models requires relevant observations, not merely a\ncurve that looks plausible.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_087_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Force–speed graph of linear and quadratic curves with numerical comparison table.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Force–speed graph of linear and quadratic\ncurves with numerical comparison table.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Linear and quadratic drag agree at one point but not over the\nfull speed range.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For a hypothetical falling object with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.20\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=0.08\\,\\mathrm{kg/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nweight is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg=2\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.32<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D=0.08\\cdot2^2=0.32\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the net downward force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.68<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.68\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndrag is 2 N so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe predicted terminal speed is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.08<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2/0.08}=5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Synthetic model comparison:<\/strong> Plot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D=0.4v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D=0.08v^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin newtons for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0,1,2,3,4,5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth give 2 N at 5 m/s but differ in between. Explain why agreement at\none point is insufficient to confirm a model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=0.08\\,\\mathrm{kg/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">cv^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen speed doubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does force balance mean at terminal speed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should an approximately constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_kN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlaw not automatically model air drag?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.72<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_D=0.08\\cdot9=0.72\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its magnitude becomes four times larger.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg=F_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile velocity remains constant and nonzero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Air drag often depends strongly on relative speed, flow regime,\nshape and fluid density.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Linear and quadratic drag describe different regimes, not\ninterchangeable universal laws. The coefficient can change with shape,\norientation and flow; real high-altitude drop tests are inappropriate\nfor school learners.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L5<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L7<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/332/gost_klp_ph_2022_06_07.pdf\">NRW\n– Upper-secondary Physics core curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 26 onward:\nintroductory phase, mechanics, kinematics, dynamics and circular motion;\nmandate is state-specific · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-4-drag-force-and-terminal-speed\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 6.4 Drag Force and Terminal Speed<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n6.4: velocity-dependent fluid resistance and terminal speed · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M14-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M14-L7 – Mechanics experiments and error analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Motion models and mechanics in the introductory phase<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A cart seems to accelerate. How can position–time readings test\nwhether its acceleration is approximately constant?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A good experiment starts with a <strong>precise question<\/strong>, a\ntestable hypothesis and a plan: Which variable changes, which is\nmeasured and what is kept constant? In mechanics, a <strong>secured,\nbounded<\/strong> trolley track may be used with markers and digital\ntiming. For a start from rest and assumed constant acceleration,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=x_0+\\tfrac12at^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPlotting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshould therefore yield an approximately straight line with slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA curved position–time plot alone cannot establish the model\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<p>Record units, zero settings, timing resolution and likely systematic\neffects. <strong>Random variation<\/strong> can be investigated through\nrepetitions; systematic sensor delay may persist after repeated\nmeasurements. A <strong>residual<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\mathrm{reading}-x_\\mathrm{model}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA patterned set of residuals can reveal an inadequate model. Measurement\nuncertainty should accompany a claim and is not the same thing as the\nmaximum residual.<\/p>\n<p>The following series is <strong>synthetic, not data from a real\nexperiment<\/strong>. It illustrates the process without pretending that\nmeasurements were made.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_088_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic observation table and position-versus-time-squared plot with an assumed reference model.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Synthetic observation table and\nposition-versus-time-squared plot with an assumed reference\nmodel.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Differences from a chosen model are residuals, not direct\nmeasurement uncertainties.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,1,2,3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntake synthetic positions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.51<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2.06<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4.61<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0,0.51,2.06,4.61\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA chosen comparison model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.51<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\mathrm{model}=0.51t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npredicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.51<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2.04<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4.59<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,0.51,2.04,4.59\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe residuals are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.02<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,0,+0.02,+0.02\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe slope in a\nposition-versus-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.51<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.51\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncorresponding to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.02<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1.02\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis value is an <strong>assumed model parameter<\/strong>, not a\nstatistically estimated experimental result. Real analysis would require\nmodel fitting, uncertainty and additional repeated measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe classroom design:<\/strong> Write a procedure for a light\ncart on a short, secured and bounded track, including end stop, three\nrepeats at each timing marker and units. Alternatively use provided or\nsynthetic data only. Make a plot of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a residual plot; contrast it with constant-velocity motion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a plot of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhelpful when acceleration is assumed constant from rest?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\mathrm{model}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.51<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\mathrm{model}=0.51t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the residual for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.06<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\mathrm{reading}=2.06\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do random scatter and systematic measurement error differ?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-x_0=\\tfrac12at^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis linear in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.51<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\mathrm{model}=0.51\\cdot4=2.04\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.06<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2.04<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.02<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2.06-2.04=+0.02\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Random effects vary between repeats; systematic effects produce a\npersistent offset or bias.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Without calibrated distances and timing, independent repeats and\ndocumented uncertainties, agreement with this chosen model does not\nestablish a reliable experimental parameter. Synthetic numbers are not\nempirical evidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite <strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L1 – Mechanical work, energy\nand conservative forces<\/a>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3.1:\nelective introductory-phase topics – projectile motion, experimentation\nand circular motion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/itl/sed/topic-areas/measurement-uncertainty\">NIST\n– Measurement Uncertainty<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Metrology guidance: measurement\nuncertainty, statistical and systematic effects · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-4-motion-with-constant-acceleration\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 1, 3.4 Motion with Constant Acceleration<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 3.4: kinematic equations and constant-acceleration assumptions ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
